final.verdict:
The major is fixed. An egg typed wider than it is long now builds, resolves and draws as the egg exactly as wide as it is long.
- **Live server:** 57x65, 57x74.1 and 11.4x220 resolve to width 57 and 11.4. They give the same solids as the as-wide-as-long egg, and the 3D shells are identical in the overlay (olcheck out 0.12 mm).
- **Thin limit:** a width under 0.55 of the length now resolves to 0.55 x length (57x8.8 builds 57x31.35).
- **Tests:** test_rg.py PASS; test_everyday.py PASS, 38 of 38 types. No built-in scenario uses the egg.
- **Rests against theory:**
  - 57x44 hen's egg rests at 188.82 deg (true 188.85).
  - 57x31.35 rests at 184.08 (true 184.09).
  - 57x57 side rest at 205.76 (true 205.66); stood on its blunt end it stays at 90.0.
  - A 57x65 egg dropped 20 cm at 45 deg on the live server ends stood on its blunt end at 90.0 deg, still by 8 s.
- **Stress sweep:** 270 drops covering 0.5 g / 57 g / 570 g, the slider corners, the clamped wide and thin eggs, 3 cm and 50 cm, 5 angles. No explosions: no speed above free fall, welds within 0.43 mm. Eggs lighter than the air they push aside (0.5 g at 140 mm and up) rise, which is correct.
- **Model:** cold build 19 to 22 ms, 9,216 triangles, at every size.

What remains is minor or belongs to other groups:
- the egg's weight does not follow its size;
- the outline is more than 1 mm off above 2x size;
- rocking is too fast and too short, because the rests are built as corners (needs an engine shape);
- small eggs are held off their rest by the engine's 0.5 mm contact margin;
- the shell sinks into the tabletop (view3d);
- the inspector shows the typed width, not the built one;
- web/sim has not been ported yet, so the browser version still builds the old egg.
